db/ref 17592186166903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428951 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428952 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428953 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428954 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1033 3189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1034 0.5153731099032455 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050801 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "68086256-0558-57ff-bb09-7a45eb02cfce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1031 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1033 3240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1034 0.4094434008891352 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "764fe6dd-703b-527f-a798-104e24a81888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation ![](images/a8/a8ea187557f2a8104688a946913572d8cd62b579c7d24dd91e4cafbfb61f9874.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157073 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/b2/b2e09fc2bc82576d702ebc07440760f80b8db1e3607888e469c3661c25ad3371.svg)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/8a/8a19755a74fe3d8c582c2408c2d802411fce6449d0e9c00bac00de8d8ca91826.svg)\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/1b/1b43b7c5f8459588632917502c96bb792a86a9f8e6c3a2d67eda71c6ce56f496.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](images/d6/d66051007ab78d2727f3b501156878a369a04b24bca337ed587feb07935a1de7.svg)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/bf/bfd809b436c24efcb2e9934b1c36293dfecb4bf87ab41c05f8cbbb9d506ea34d.svg)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157074 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/7e/7e191da3b082da420622418962e7491a6e78b6ed8192259b9b50ec8040bbc525.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/fb/fb833263882adc2422083c111e96b0e030263f93e4c0174082f0f57667b46e8e.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/fe/fe33e849a0e3baa7e8547c9559f9c36e980b8e715cba6e26900eea5d815e9b30.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/2c/2c443847a5559737066873593b399117c03e9c8141b403bc7f820c9ad493e2a9.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/00/00b635d3858d38c41ad9e1bacb00ed4c6370b5e2112aa82faa6ef37a83209a6c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/d1/d1911a797bfa5915fb1478c91dc007ca3f1419b1093180dfb08932a008337b6e.svg)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/be/be4b1ac82a25533e28028ad0aade71e2dd2614c5cd302d777a90a9f16be845c1.svg)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157075 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/dfddfc4e82f21cd490aff75bbdc6fbece98cd05a5cc70a1148492697faa8741a.svg)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/92/92c6c6be816658e065ebba1079a2c7fa031cc9137ffa8dec7bf6e498e69195f3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/75/75524e426e894f50985740367c96f128dfef1e216ae6969a529889cf22803bc7.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/bb/bb892c2fd7b1a2873b9a7427d95599f67fbd10e47ec6fd2cb377b9f6f45f4185.svg)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/46/4696fe4ee3fd46a639fb465c10aa2ac44e608a0cf59ca6c1a210eb4861b3dd5b.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/05/0525d47967c203832ff4deea0931e007cc7127f24d248cd8265765ad95708367.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/48/48da1d81a447aa78c3ffa68ad5f089bc0d8e4bac5fa489ddad2747055b0f2a99.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-2(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157445 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157446 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186530529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157447 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157476 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c2c46d67-88f2-5cb5-ad0b-e155cb945332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157737 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157737 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157737 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157737 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157737 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f38771d6-22f6-588d-95e5-d42dd40123e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157737 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1a7a31a-a1ff-52db-b87c-67b3aaf6bbd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157783 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1061 550835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb2bbd4a-aeae-553c-ac02-210278766cbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158391 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1061 550836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59fd701c-e0be-5da0-8006-b206fcd15119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158392 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1061 550839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf1cdfb1-e3d1-56f0-b6a6-775a91e83782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158393 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1061 550842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b9a873f-47e9-56dd-84a0-2bf953ccd698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158394 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158395 #edb/ref 1061 550845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158395 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37b8ee3e-0296-5c88-ad97-3614e478781f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158395 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1064 "For a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nwe know that if $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with the corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{black}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\lambda_2},$ respectively, then the general solution formula is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut what happens if $A$ has a single repeated eigenvalue, $\\lambda?$ It depends on the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **complete** eigenvalue if there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **defective** (or **incomplete**) eigenvalue if there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\nThis leads to two distinct cases:\n\n- *The Complete Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *complete* eigenvalue, then there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda,$ and we can use the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t}\n$$\n\n- *The Defective Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *defective* eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v.$ We cannot use the usual formula because there are not enough eigenvectors.\n\nInstead, we must find a *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf w,$ which is a vector that satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is then given by the formula: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, )\n$$\n\nWe'll explain how and why this solution comes about at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1065 4281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "76c4a128-aa18-5cb4-a460-0811386673d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158467 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nin the case where $A$ has a single, defective eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the first fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t},\n$$\n\nas usual, and the second fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and $\\mathbf w$ is a vector that satisfies $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nWe can see that the second fundamental solution works by substituting $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{′}(t) & =A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}] & =A(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}) \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Avt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v+\\lambda w & =Aw \\\\ v & =Aw-\\lambda w \\\\ v & =(A-\\lambda I)w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we conclude that $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ works as a second fundamental solution provided that $\\mathbf w$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{v}  =  (A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{w}.$\n\n**Note:**\nBy linearity, any linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ is also a solution of the system. Moreover, it turns out, every solution can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t).$ Later in the course, we’ll see why certain particular solutions can generate the entire solution set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1065 4282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "044881e6-feac-543e-b223-ea1a1c9da634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158468 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification for the Solution Formula in the Defective Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299983 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-6t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186299983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09b833ac-6e99-5f04-9aea-02a1d1abf1cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300185 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e2f4047-28db-5982-bf27-62bd47c9eb17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300197 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186300197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30e58490-02de-57c1-a235-c5f21bc66f4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300272 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186300272 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300272 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f187179-b0f8-5d94-91aa-4179df6e4772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300290 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42e4a570-7f1b-547f-b489-c83eaeafeeac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300354 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300354 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300354 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b99766b-da02-5b51-a022-313d44a16524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300595 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f8e3287-39f8-532f-b6b0-a87ef89feb4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300623 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}(-3-12t){e}^{5t} \\\\ 2\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186300623 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300623 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "848d9602-7869-51e2-9531-be656bfae476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301066 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186301066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dea94c08-d4a8-50f4-b269-2013de24496f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301086 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e27d740d-be6d-5d25-b6a0-e68ad85f227e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186299983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186301066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186301066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301713 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9741ff31-5e13-50b4-8cd7-e54eafbd4045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186301086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301863 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2e08cf9-f383-515d-a856-21fa02542b1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302268 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186302268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26bb41ab-79e8-5c9f-95c2-ae20713f031a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302268 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince $A$ is lower-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 4{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\\,t{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\,{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2-12t)\\,{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345621 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e9a8c81-a091-545d-84fc-e0dd993ec18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345932 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186345932 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345932 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "434dca2b-bb1e-5b6a-a0fc-484d397d614c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346102 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186346102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "581d572d-6189-5f30-8179-ad07a3ead556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346288 #edb/ref 1080 "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{