ght.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+5(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394084 #edb