9 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413831 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413831 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413831 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413831 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413831 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413832 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186413832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413832 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186428764 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428764 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: enc